    {
      "title": "Delivering India’s National Statement at the UN General Assembly, External Affairs Minister S. Jaishankar on Saturday said the country will exercise its right to defend itself against terrorism while focusing on the impact of conflicts on the Global South.",
      "location": "Column 4, Vertical 345.0-350.8"
    },
    {
      "title": "In a response to Pakistan Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif’s speech a day before, where he had criticised India for launching Operation Sindoor last year, Mr. Jaishankar said that there could be “no dilution” of the world’s zero-tolerance approach to terrorism. “Yesterday, a serial practitioner of terrorism misrepresented facts before this Assembly. Arguments were invoked to normalise terrorism and claim immunity from its consequences,” Mr. Jaishankar said, attacking Mr. Sharif’s speech, in which he had called Kashmir the “jugular vein” of Pakistan and accused India of human rights violations in the region. Mr. Sharif had also claimed that the BJP government in India unleashed a “reign of terror” against minorities in the country.",
      "location": "Column 4, Vertical 372.5-378.3"
    },
    {
